namespace Death {
//###==##====#=====--==~--~=~- --- -- -  -  -   -

/** @page shader-compiler Shader compilation
@brief Offline shader preprocessing tool of Jazz² Resurrection

@tableofcontents

@m_class{m-noindent}

ShaderCompiler (@cpp "Sources/Utilities/ShaderCompiler" @ce, dependency-free C++17) reads annotated
`.shader` files from @cpp "Sources/Shaders" @ce, expands their variants, reflects the GLSL declarations
offline and emits self-contained C++ headers with sources and reflection data, so the runtime needs no
`glGetActiveUniform` introspection. It also performs every lowering the non-GL backends need: HLSL,
Vulkan GLSL + SPIR-V, Metal, ESSL 100, Cg, GLSL-to-C++ for the software renderer, and a fixed-function
transpiler for backends without fragment shaders @m_span{m-text m-dim} (PVR, GX, PICA, GU, GS, RDP,
LegacyGL) @m_endspan.

A `.shader` file describes an effect on two tiers:

-   <b>Shader backends</b> (software renderer included, via @ref ShaderCompiler::GlslToCpp) get the
    GLSL of @cpp void vertex() @ce / @cpp void fragment() @ce, lowered per variant and per backend.
-   <b>Fixed-function backends</b> get a @cpp void fixed_function() @ce block --- hardware passes over
    the sprite quad, transpiled to C++. Design: @cpp "Docs/FixedFunctionShaderDesign.md" @ce; runtime
    contract: @cpp "Sources/nCine/Graphics/RHI/FixedFunctionPass.h" @ce.

All outputs are <b>committed</b> under @cpp "Sources/Shaders/Generated" @ce; the game's build never runs
the tool, so regenerate after every shader edit, see @ref shader-compiler-workflow.

@section shader-compiler-artifacts Which artifact serves which platform

One per-shader header serves GL, ES2, D3D11 and Vulkan; the other tiers get aggregate headers. The
committed set must only be produced by @ref shader-compiler-workflow "`--generate-all`", which runs
every mode below.

Backend | Artifact | Produced by
------- | -------- | -----------
OpenGL 3.3, OpenGL\|ES 3.0, WebGL 2.0 | `VsSource` / `FsSource` of the per-shader header @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`#version` and platform defines injected at runtime) @m_endspan | primary mode
OpenGL\|ES 2.0 profile (`NCINE_RHI_GL_PROFILE=ES2`) | `VsSource100` / `FsSource100` --- ESSL 100 lowering in the same header | primary mode
Direct3D 11 | `HlslVsSource` / `HlslFsSource` --- Shader Model 4/5 HLSL in the same header | primary mode
Vulkan | `VkVsSpirv` / `VkFsSpirv` --- SPIR-V compiled offline by `glslangValidator` | primary mode + glslang
Metal (macOS) | `MslVsSource` / `MslFsSource` --- compiled on the device at load time (no MSL compiler outside Xcode) | primary mode
Software renderer | @cpp "SwGeneratedShaders.h" @ce --- transpiled C++ fragment functions | `--emit-sw-generated`
Dreamcast (PVR) | @cpp "PvrGeneratedEffects.h" @ce | `--emit-fixed-function pvr`
Wii / GameCube (GX) | @cpp "GxGeneratedEffects.h" @ce | `--emit-fixed-function gx`
Nintendo 3DS (PICA) | @cpp "PicaGeneratedEffects.h" @ce | `--emit-fixed-function pica`
PlayStation Portable (GU) | @cpp "GuGeneratedEffects.h" @ce | `--emit-fixed-function gu`
PlayStation 2 (GS) | @cpp "GsGeneratedEffects.h" @ce | `--emit-fixed-function gs`
Nintendo 64 (RDP) | @cpp "RdpGeneratedEffects.h" @ce | `--emit-fixed-function rdp`
Legacy OpenGL 1.x @m_span{m-text m-dim} (MorphOS' TinyGL) @m_endspan | @cpp "LegacyGlGeneratedEffects.h" @ce | `--emit-fixed-function legacygl`
PlayStation Vita (GXM) | @cpp "CgGeneratedShaders.h" @ce --- Cg sources compiled on the console | `--emit-cg`
PlayStation 3 (RSX) | @cpp "RsxGeneratedShaders.h" @ce --- NV40 microcode compiled offline from the same Cg | `--emit-rsx` + cgcomp

@m_class{m-noindent}

The `*GeneratedEffects.h` headers hold transpiled `fixed_function` effects. Unavailable lowerings are
null (a construct outside the HLSL/ESSL 100 subset, or no glslang for SPIR-V). A program without a
`fixed_function` block is skipped on those backends with a one-time warning; a shader the software
transpiler declines is absent from @cpp "SwGeneratedShaders.h" @ce.

Runtime-compiled `.shader` files (@ref ShaderCompiler::RuntimeProgram, `CompileRuntimeProgram`) get
only GLSL, reflection and, under ES2, the ESSL 100 lowering --- so they are unavailable on the
fixed-function backends.

@section shader-compiler-building Building the tool

The desktop CMake build adds the tool as a utility target in the `Utilities` folder. It is skipped when
cross-compiling @m_span{m-text m-dim} (Emscripten, Nintendo Switch, Vita, UWP, Wii, GameCube,
Dreamcast) @m_endspan, since it runs on the build machine. Standalone build:

@code{.sh}
cmake -S Sources/Utilities/ShaderCompiler -B build-shadercompiler
cmake --build build-shadercompiler
@endcode

@section shader-compiler-usage Command-line reference

@code{.sh}
ShaderCompiler <input.shader> -o <output.h> [-n <namespace>] [--glslang <path>]
ShaderCompiler <input.shader> --check | --essl100-check | --hlsl | --cg | --vulkan | --msl
ShaderCompiler --generate-all [--shaders-dir <dir>] [--out-dir <dir>] [--check] [--no-dxbc] [--glslang <path>]
ShaderCompiler --emit-types <output.h>
ShaderCompiler --emit-sw-generated <output.h> <input.shader ...>
ShaderCompiler --emit-cg <output.h> <input.shader ...>
ShaderCompiler --emit-fixed-function <pvr|gx|pica|gu|gs|rdp|legacygl> <output.h> <input.shader ...>
ShaderCompiler --hlsl-check <input.shader ...>
ShaderCompiler --spirv-check [--glslang <path>] <input.shader ...>
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

The standalone modes (`--generate-all`, `--emit-*`, `--hlsl-check`, `--spirv-check`) are recognized
only as the first argument; elsewhere they are an unknown option. Everything else is the primary
per-shader mode, which takes exactly one input file.

Mode or option | Meaning
-------------- | -------
`-o <output.h>` | Generated C++ header. Required unless a dump switch is given
`-n <namespace>` | Namespace for the program <b>data</b> (default `ShaderArtifacts`); `::` nesting allowed; empty, a leading digit or a character outside `[A-Za-z0-9_:]` is an error
`--glslang <path>` | `glslangValidator` for the embedded SPIR-V; otherwise found via `VULKAN_SDK\Bin`, `VULKAN_SDK\Bin32` and `PATH`
`--check` | Reflection dump of every program and variant (structs with std140 offsets, uniforms, blocks with `BaseSize`/`InstanceStride`, texture units, attribute locations); the test suite compares it against @cpp "tests/expected" @ce
`--essl100-check`, `--target essl100` | ESSL 100 (OpenGL\|ES 2.0) transform of every variant; `essl100` is the only `--target` value
`--hlsl` | HLSL (Shader Model 4/5) transform
`--cg` | Cg transform in the PS Vita's sceGxm dialect, see @ref shader-compiler-cg "below"
`--vulkan` | Vulkan GLSL (@cpp "#version 450" @ce) transform; needs no glslang
`--msl` | Metal Shading Language transform
`--generate-all` | Regenerates <b>every</b> committed artifact, see @ref shader-compiler-workflow "below". `--shaders-dir` / `--out-dir` override the auto-detected directories, `--check` compares instead of writing, `--no-dxbc` and `--glslang` are forwarded
`--emit-types <output.h>` | Writes only the shared reflection-types header
`--emit-sw-generated <output.h> <in ...>` | Software-renderer header: every variant's fragment stage as C++
`--emit-cg <output.h> <in ...>` | PS Vita header: every variant as Cg
`--emit-rsx <output.h> <in ...>` | PlayStation 3 header: every variant as Cg compiled by `cgcomp` (path via `--cgcomp`). The only path building with `NO_DYNAMIC_BRANCHING`
`--emit-fixed-function <pvr\|gx\|pica\|gu\|gs\|rdp\|legacygl> <output.h> <in ...>` | Per-backend effect header: the applicable `fixed_function` block of every variant
`--hlsl-check <in ...>` | Compiles every variant's HLSL via `D3DCompile` (`vs_5_0` / `ps_5_0`), printing a pass/fail table @m_span{m-text m-dim} (Windows only, `d3dcompiler_47.dll`) @m_endspan
`--spirv-check [--glslang <path>] <in ...>` | Compiles every variant's Vulkan GLSL to SPIR-V via `glslangValidator`, printing a pass/fail table
`--help`, `-h`, `/?` | Prints usage to stderr and exits successfully

@m_class{m-noindent}

The dump switches (`--check`, `--essl100-check`, `--hlsl`, `--cg`, `--vulkan`, `--msl`) print to
stdout and write nothing. When several are combined, the first of `--essl100-check`, `--hlsl`, `--cg`,
`--vulkan`, `--msl`, `--check` wins and the rest are silently ignored; `--glslang` is ignored by all of
them.

Diagnostics go to stderr as @cpp "<file>:<line>: error: <message>" @ce (parser, reflection, emitters),
@cpp "<file>: error: <message>" @ce (failed `#include` or unreadable input) or a bare
@cpp "error: <message>" @ce (command line and I/O). Exit code: `0` success, `1` input or emission
failure, `2` usage error.

@m_class{m-block m-warning}

@par Windows-only integrations
`D3DCompile` and `glslangValidator` are invoked through Windows APIs, so `--hlsl-check` and
`--spirv-check` fail elsewhere and the primary mode silently emits null SPIR-V. The transforms
themselves are portable, but a full regeneration has to happen on Windows.

@subsection shader-compiler-usage-aggregate The aggregate modes

-   `--emit-types`: the types live in the fixed @cpp ShaderCompiler @ce namespace, independent of `-n`,
    so headers with different data namespaces can be included together.
-   `--emit-sw-generated` and `--emit-cg` never fail on unsupported input: the shader or variant is
    <b>declined</b>, omitted and listed with its reason in the summary (the Vita backend then reports it
    unavailable at load time). `--emit-sw-generated` is the only path building with `SOFTWARE_RENDERER`.
-   Cg is a separate header because the per-shader headers can only be rewritten on Windows (DXBC,
    SPIR-V); a Cg run touches nothing else.
-   `--emit-fixed-function` treats an invalid block as a <b>hard error</b> that fails the whole run.

@section shader-compiler-input The .shader input format

A `.shader` file is GLSL globals plus @cpp void vertex() @ce / @cpp void fragment() @ce entry points,
annotated with keyword directives --- top-level statements at brace depth 0, terminated by `;`.
Comments follow GLSL rules and pass through into the emitted sources. Do <b>not</b> write
@cpp #version @ce --- the engine injects the version header (@cpp "#version 330" @ce,
@cpp "#version 300 es" @ce, @cpp "#version 100" @ce) and platform defines at runtime. The full in-tree
specification, including std140 layout rules and the `--check` dump format, is
@cpp "Sources/Utilities/ShaderCompiler/README.md" @ce.

@subsection shader-compiler-input-directives Top-level directives

Directive | Meaning
--------- | -------
`program <Name>;` | <b>Required, exactly once</b>, before `shader_type`, `variant`, `batched`, `precision` and the entry points --- write it first. A C++/GLSL identifier naming the emitted `Program` (and keying the console tables)
`shader_type canvas_item;` `shader_type custom;` | Optional, default `custom`. `canvas_item` opts into the sprite template
`variant <NAME>;` | May repeat. Output holds the unnamed base variant (`Variants[0]`) plus one entry per declaration, compiled with `#define <NAME> (1)`. <b>No cross-products</b>
`render_mode <mode>[, <mode>];` | Any of `blend_mix`, `blend_add`, `blend_sub`, `blend_mul`, `blend_premul_alpha`, `unshaded`, OR-ed into `RenderModes`. May repeat
`precision mediump;` `precision highp;` | Optional (default `mediump`), only these spellings: precision of the auto-emitted @cpp "#ifdef GL_ES" @ce fragment prologue (`precision highp float;` is plain GLSL). `highp` is guarded by @cpp "GL_FRAGMENT_PRECISION_HIGH" @ce, falling back to `mediump` on ES 2.0 devices without it
`batched <Name>;` | `canvas_item` only --- also emits the batched twin (`InstancesBlock` + 6-vertex corner formula), sharing the fragment stage, variants and `fixed_function` blocks
`#include "relative/path"` | Replaced <b>textually</b> before parsing, relative to the including file, up to depth 8. Diagnostic line numbers refer to the expanded stream
`void fixed_function([<target>[, <target>...]]) { ... }` | Fixed-function implementation, see @ref shader-compiler-fixed-function "below". Never part of the GLSL stages

@subsection shader-compiler-input-declarations Declarations shared by both modes

Element | Meaning
------- | -------
`varying [flat] [precision] <type> <name>;` | Becomes @cpp out @ce in the vertex stage and @cpp in @ce in the fragment stage, qualifiers preserved. May be wrapped in a global-scope `SOFTWARE_RENDERER` or `LOW_POWER_GPU` conditional, see @ref shader-compiler-input-preprocessor "below"
`attribute [layout(location = N)] <declaration>;` | Vertex attribute --- an @cpp in @ce global in the <b>vertex stage only</b>; a leading `layout(...)` is kept and honored by reflection
`uniform <type> <name> : <hint>[, <hint>];` | `texture_unit(N)` (<b>0--31</b>) is the <b>only</b> explicit texture-unit assignment; the name must match a sampler in some variant, and two hints for one uniform are an error. `source_color`, `hint_range(...)`, `filter_nearest`, `filter_linear`, `repeat_enable`, `repeat_disable` are parsed and dropped; anything else is an error
everything else | Uniforms, std140 blocks, consts, structs and helper functions are shared by both stages of every variant

@m_class{m-block m-warning}

@par Shared std140 blocks and precision
    A named `std140` block reaches both stages, and GLSL ES requires block members to match in
    precision across stages. The vertex stage defaults `float` to `highp`, so a `mediump` fragment
    prologue makes the program fail to link (on WebGL 2.0: "precisions of uniform block member differ").
    Declare `precision highp;` in such a shader, or qualify the block members explicitly.

@subsection shader-compiler-input-custom Custom mode (the default)

No template, no substitutions; both entry points are required. The body of @cpp void vertex() @ce
becomes the vertex @cpp main() @ce verbatim and writes @cpp gl_Position @ce itself. In
@cpp void fragment() @ce, @cpp COLOR @ce is the output variable itself (@cpp "out vec4 COLOR;" @ce),
undefined until written, so an early @cpp return; @ce is safe. `fragColor` anywhere in a `.shader` file
is a parse error --- write @cpp COLOR @ce.

@subsection shader-compiler-input-canvas Canvas mode (shader_type canvas_item)

Uses the engine's sprite template (`InstanceBlock` / `InstancesBlock`, the `vTexCoords` / `vColor` /
`vPaletteOffset` varyings) and enables `batched`.

-   @cpp void vertex() @ce is optional. The generated @cpp main() @ce computes the built-ins as locals,
    splices the body, then runs the standard epilogue, so @cpp return; @ce in it is a parse error. It can
    be given to the low-power emissions alone via a global-scope `#if LOW_POWER_GPU`, see
    @ref shader-compiler-input-preprocessor.
-   @cpp void fragment() @ce is required and enters with @cpp COLOR @ce holding the instance colour.

Built-in | Vertex stage | Fragment stage
-------- | ------------ | --------------
`VERTEX` | sprite-local position in pixels, writable | ---
`UV` | texture coordinates, writable | substituted with `vTexCoords`
`COLOR` | instance colour, writable | enters as the instance colour, and is the output
`PALETTE_OFFSET` | palette row of the instance, writable | substituted with `vPaletteOffset`
`TEXTURE` | --- | substituted with `uTexture`

@m_class{m-noindent}

-   @cpp TEXTURE @ce without a `uTexture` declaration auto-declares
    @cpp "uniform sampler2D uTexture;" @ce on unit 0.
-   @cpp NORMAL @ce, @cpp SCREEN_UV @ce, @cpp SCREEN_PIXEL_SIZE @ce, @cpp TIME @ce,
    @cpp POINT_COORD @ce and @cpp VERTEX @ce are errors in @cpp void fragment() @ce and shared globals.
-   Built-ins are locals of the generated @cpp main() @ce; redeclaring one is a GLSL error.
-   Without @cpp void vertex() @ce the vertex stage is the template alone, without shared globals.

@subsection shader-compiler-input-preprocessor Variants, conditionals and includes

Reflection runs per variant through a mini preprocessor implementing the object-like C subset
(@cpp #define @ce / @cpp #undef @ce, @cpp #if @ce / @cpp #ifdef @ce / @cpp #ifndef @ce /
@cpp #elif @ce / @cpp #else @ce / @cpp #endif @ce with integer constant expressions). It only feeds
reflection: emitted sources keep the original text verbatim, with the variant define and
@cpp "#line 1" @ce at the top. `GL_ES` is never predefined (reflection uses the desktop GL view);
`BATCH_SIZE` evaluates as 1 in @cpp #if @ce but stays symbolic as an array size.

These macros are resolved by the tool and never survive into an emitted source:

Macro | Defined for | Use
----- | ----------- | ---
`VERTEX_STAGE` / `FRAGMENT_STAGE` | the respective stage, at assembly time | which stage sees a shared global (optional @cpp "#else" @ce). Rarely needed: attributes and varyings have keywords, unused functions are eliminated per stage
`SOFTWARE_RENDERER` | `--emit-sw-generated` only | a cheaper CPU path that leaves every other backend unchanged (@cpp "TexturedBackground.shader" @ce)
`NO_DYNAMIC_BRANCHING` | `--emit-rsx` only | gate every dynamically branching fragment block on it --- NV40 `IF`/`LOOP`/`BRK` miscompiles (the branch body overwrites live registers)
`LOW_POWER_GPU` | `--emit-cg` (PS Vita) and the ESSL 100 lowering | a cheaper approximation of an expensive path, not a capability limit. In a per-shader header only `_Vs100`/`_Fs100` are built with it; the engine's `RHI_LOW_POWER_GPU` (`RhiFwd.h`) mirrors which view a binary compiled

@m_class{m-noindent}

-   They work in @cpp #ifdef @ce / @cpp #ifndef @ce <b>and</b> @cpp #if @ce / @cpp #elif @ce
    expressions (`#if !SOFTWARE_RENDERER && !NO_DYNAMIC_BRANCHING`): each family folds the top-level
    @cpp && @ce terms naming only its macros and leaves the rest. Defining or undefining them is an
    error. Variant names resolve per variant the same way, also in @cpp fixed_function @ce blocks.
-   Around <b>global-scope `varying` declarations</b>, `SOFTWARE_RENDERER` / `LOW_POWER_GPU` give those
    emissions their own varyings. Such a conditional may hold only `varying` declarations, no nested
    directives --- except that its `LOW_POWER_GPU` side may also hold a `canvas_item` shader's
    @cpp vertex() @ce (@cpp "Blur.shader" @ce). See @cpp "tests/SwVarying.shader" @ce,
    @cpp "tests/LowPowerVarying.shader" @ce, @cpp "tests/LowPowerCanvasVertex.shader" @ce.
-   When a low-power vertex stage replaces derivatives with matrix data
    (@cpp "TileMapMeshBlur.shader" @ce), take only the <em>magnitude</em>: the projection's y sign
    differs between backends (GL flips it while a render target is bound).

@m_class{m-block m-warning}

@par Conditionals that survive into an emitted source
A surviving @cpp #if @ce is real GLSL, and <b>GLSL ES rejects an undefined macro in an `#if`</b> ---
desktop GLSL, HLSL and the `#version 450` Vulkan transform accept it, so `--hlsl-check` and
`--spirv-check` do not catch it; a macro with an <b>empty</b> body (@cpp "#if SLOPE" @ce) fails even on
desktop. The tool therefore lowers a purely boolean surviving @cpp #if @ce: one term becomes
@cpp #ifdef @ce / @cpp #ifndef @ce (@cpp "#if DITHER && !SOFTWARE_RENDERER" @ce emits
@cpp "#ifdef DITHER" @ce), longer ones wrap each flag in @cpp defined(...) @ce. `tests/GlslSweep.ps1`
compiles every generated stage source in all three injected profiles and catches anything that slips
through.

@subsection shader-compiler-input-cleanup Automatic cleanup passes

Run per stage, in this order:

1.  Stage conditionals are resolved.
2.  Unused functions are removed, to a fixpoint rooted at @cpp main() @ce.
3.  A varying the fragment stage never reads is removed with its dead vertex-stage stores, or demoted
    to a plain global when a store has side effects.
4.  Unused uniforms, blocks, defines and structs are removed from a stage only when the same
    declaration survives in the other stage, so reflection stays byte-identical.
5.  The same rule applies per backend view: a loose uniform read only by branches that view drops
    (`SOFTWARE_RENDERER` / `NO_DYNAMIC_BRANCHING` / `LOW_POWER_GPU`) leaves that stage.
6.  Literal-only subexpressions inside function bodies are constant-folded with exact GLSL semantics;
    global declarations are never touched.

@section shader-compiler-output Reflection and generated headers

Reflection runs per stage and variant over global declarations (structs, loose uniforms,
@cpp "layout (std140)" @ce blocks with computed offsets and strides, `sampler2D` / `sampler3D` /
`samplerCube` bindings, vertex attributes) and the stages are merged GL-style. An array sized by
`BATCH_SIZE` records its element stride as the block's `InstanceStride` with a sentinel count; the
runtime computes the batch size as `maxUniformBlockSize / InstanceStride` @m_span{m-text m-dim}
(the batched sprite instance's 112-byte stride gives @cpp 585 = 65536 / 112 @ce) @m_endspan.

Each header is self-contained (`<cstdint>` / `<cstddef>` plus the types header) and carries per variant:

-   <b>Stage sources</b> as raw string literals --- `<Program>_Vs` / `_Fs`, or
    `<Program>_<VARIANT>_Vs` / `_Fs` --- each starting with the variant define and @cpp "#line 1" @ce
-   The <b>ESSL 100</b> and <b>HLSL</b> lowerings (`_Vs100` / `_Fs100`, `_HlslVs` / `_HlslFs`), and
    <b>SPIR-V</b> words when glslang was available
-   <b>Reflection</b> as @cpp constexpr @ce arrays (`Uniform`, `BlockMember`, `UniformBlock`,
    `TextureBinding`, `Attribute`), tied together by `ProgramVariant` and `Program`

The types live in @cpp "Generated/ShaderCompilerTypes.h" @ce in the fixed @cpp ShaderCompiler @ce
namespace, data in the `-n` namespace; engine code can include the types header alone.

@section shader-compiler-fixed-function Fixed-function blocks

On backends without fragment shaders an effect is a list of passes over the sprite quad, stated by a
@cpp fixed_function @ce block next to the GLSL it approximates. The whole block of
@cpp "WhiteMask.shader" @ce:

@code{.cpp}
void fixed_function() {
	pass p;
	p.color = vec4(0.0, 0.0, 0.0, COLOR.a);
	p.offset_color = COLOR.rgb;
	submit_quad(p);
}
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

Anything outside the grammar is a <b>hard error</b> with file and line that fails the whole
`--generate-all` run. Design and per-console capabilities: @cpp "Docs/FixedFunctionShaderDesign.md" @ce;
emitter: @ref ShaderCompiler::ConsoleFixedFunction.

@subsection shader-compiler-ff-selection Block selection and what gets emitted

A block's parentheses hold its target list:

Spelling | Serves
-------- | ------
@cpp void fixed_function() @ce | every backend without a more specific block; restricted to the portable core
@cpp void fixed_function(pvr) @ce | Dreamcast
@cpp void fixed_function(gx) @ce | Wii/GameCube
@cpp void fixed_function(pica) @ce | Nintendo 3DS
@cpp void fixed_function(gu) @ce | PlayStation Portable
@cpp void fixed_function(gs) @ce | PlayStation 2
@cpp void fixed_function(rdp) @ce | Nintendo 64
@cpp void fixed_function(legacygl) @ce | OpenGL 1.x @m_span{m-text m-dim} (MorphOS' TinyGL, or desktop GL in a compatibility context) @m_endspan
@cpp void fixed_function(pvr, gu, gs) @ce | every listed target from one body; any subset, any order, whitespace free

@m_class{m-noindent}

-   A block naming a backend (alone or in a list) wins over the generic block regardless of order.
-   Each target belongs to exactly <b>one</b> block per file; claiming it twice is an error.
-   Use a list only when the code is literally identical; capabilities are checked against the
    @ref shader-compiler-ff-target-lists "intersection of the listed targets".
-   A program with no applicable block is skipped on that backend at runtime.

Each variant's applicable block becomes a
@cpp "void <Program>[_<VARIANT>]_Effect(EffectContext&)" @ce function, listed in a
`FixedFunctionGeneratedEffects[]` table of

@code{.cpp}
{ program, variant, usesOffsetColor, requirements, intrinsic, &function }
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

entries keyed by the (program, variant) identity from `ShaderProgram::SetProgramIdentity()`; an entry
has a function or an intrinsic, never both. The including device file supplies `EffectContext` through
a @cpp using @ce alias before the include.

-   `usesOffsetColor` --- whether a reachable `p.offset_color = ...` exists (the PVR enables specular
    per program).
-   `requirements` --- `FixedFunctionRequirements` bits for the context facilities used:
    `NeedsTexelStep` (`texel_size()` / `has_texel_size()`), `NeedsUniforms` (`has_uniform()` /
    `uniform_vec2/vec4()`), `NeedsStripBuilder` (`strip_*()` / `submit_strip[_shaded]()`),
    `NeedsQuadAxes` (`quad_origin()` / `quad_axis_x/y()`).

Both are computed from the block selected for that backend. Byte-identical bodies are emitted once,
with a `from <file>:<block>` line and a `// Shared by:` list.

@subsection shader-compiler-ff-pipeline Pipeline bindings

A block whose <b>sole</b> statement is `pipeline <name>;` binds the program to a backend pipeline stage
consuming an engine data structure; the table entry carries a `FixedFunctionIntrinsic` instead of a
function. Anything else in such a block is an error.

`pipeline` name | `FixedFunctionIntrinsic` | Stage | Declared by
--------------- | ------------------------ | ----- | -----------
`tile_map_mesh` | `TileMapMesh` | a whole tile layer as one indexed triangle-list mesh (8-float `TileMap::AppendTileQuad` contract, corners addressed through @cpp RenderResources::GetQuadIndices() @ce, resolved on the CPU; PVR scissor-clips) | `TileMapMesh.shader`, `TileMapMeshPalette.shader`
`lighting_combine` | `LightingCombine` | viewport compositor --- the direct-tier CPU-lightmap lighting hook | `Combine.shader`, `CombineWithWater.shader`, `CombineWithWaterLow.shader`
`line_strip_mesh` | `LineStripMesh` | vertex-fed textured line strip of the weapon wheel (PVR expands to thin quads, GX/GU draw native lines) | `DefaultMeshSprite.shader`

@m_class{m-noindent}

Geometry-synthesized effects (transition iris, warped background) are ordinary blocks on the strip
builder, not intrinsics.

@subsection shader-compiler-ff-pass Passes and their fields

`pass p;` declares a `FixedFunctionPass` with engine defaults (plain modulated sprite, material
blending). No initializer, one statement per pass; fields are <b>write-only</b> and take only plain `=`.

Field | Type | Meaning
----- | ---- | -------
`p.color` | `vec4` | per-vertex colour of the pass
`p.offset_color` | `vec3` | post-texture additive term. Writing it enables it and sets `usesOffsetColor`. GX uses a silhouette pass; GU expands it into modulate + additive silhouette (same result, one draw when `color.rgb` is zero), so generic blocks using it work everywhere
`p.screen_offset` | `vec2` | displacement of the whole quad in its own coordinate space
`p.luma_gain` | `float` | `LUMA_RAMP` luminance amplification before saturation; ignored by other presets
`p.texture_repeat` | `bool` literal | `fixed_function(rdp)` blocks only: hardware wrap along S, so coordinates outside [0, 1] repeat instead of the strip being cut. Only adds wrapping; GE and PICA200 take wrap from the sampler state
`p.blend` | enum | blend override for this pass
`p.tev` | enum | combiner preset --- portable intent; the PVR always modulates, the GE knows only `MODULATE` and `SILHOUETTE`

`p.blend` | `BlendMode` | Hardware
--------- | ----------- | --------
`MATERIAL` | `Material` | whatever the material configured (default)
`ADD` | `Additive` | additive glow and split-multiplier passes; GX `ONE + ONE`, PVR `SRCALPHA + ONE` (scaled by pass alpha)
`OPAQUE` | `Opaque` | `ONE + ZERO`
`ALPHA` | `Alpha` | `SRCALPHA + INVSRCALPHA`, independent of the material

`p.tev` | `TevPreset` | Meaning and availability
------- | ----------- | ------------------------
`MODULATE` | `Modulate` | `texture * vertex colour` (default)
`SILHOUETTE` | `Silhouette` | vertex colour where the texture has alpha (masks, shadows, glows)
`MODULATE_X2` | `ModulateX2` | modulate with x2 output scale. <b>Rejected in any block reaching `gu` or `gs`</b> (no output scale); RDP via its second combiner cycle, LegacyGL via `GL_RGB_SCALE`
`MODULATE_X4` | `ModulateX4` | modulate with x4 output scale. Rejected for `gu`, `gs` <b>and `rdp`</b> (would need a third combiner cycle)
`TINT_MIX` | `TintMix` | `mix(texel, colour, alpha)`, opaque result. Needs a lerping combiner: only blocks whose targets are all among `gx`, `pica`, `rdp`, `legacygl`
`LUMA_RAMP` | `LumaRamp` | silhouette toned per texel: `color.rgb` at luminance 0, `offset_color` at 1, `luma_gain` amplifying the Rec.601 luminance; coverage `texel alpha * color.a`. <b>Only in a block targeting `gx` alone</b>

@m_class{m-noindent}

These checks reject the generic block too. The PVR silently ignores `p.tev` (an output scale has no
effect there); without a combiner, express a boost as extra additive passes (`Colorized.shader`).

@subsection shader-compiler-ff-builtins Built-ins

The <b>portable core</b> is valid in every block. The <b>extended</b> vocabulary is only valid in blocks
that name their targets (single or list), never in the generic block.

Built-in | Type | Meaning | Core?
-------- | ---- | ------- | -----
`COLOR` | `vec4` | instance colour of the draw, the shader's `COLOR` input. Read-only, cannot be redeclared | yes
`texel_size()` | `vec2` | one texel's displacement in the quad's coordinate space, converted per backend | yes
`has_texel_size()` | `bool` | whether that step is derivable (a zero `texRect` has none); guard `texel_size()` with it | yes
`submit_quad(p)` | statement | submits one pass over the current instance's quad | yes
`quad_origin()` | `vec2` | <b>pre-clip</b> raster position of the sprite's (0,0) corner | no
`quad_axis_x()`, `quad_axis_y()` | `vec2` | pre-clip raster displacements of the sprite's local axes; use these for synthesized geometry so clipping cannot distort it | no
`has_uniform(uName)` | `bool` | whether the program resolved that uniform (unresolved ones load zeros) | no
`uniform_vec2(uName)`, `uniform_vec4(uName)` | `vec2`, `vec4` | resolved uniform by name via `ResolveUniform`. The argument is an <b>identifier</b>, not a string | no
`strip_position(i, <vec2>)` | statement | strip vertex position | no
`strip_uv(i, <vec2>)` | statement | strip vertex UV in the shader's texture space | no
`strip_color(i, <vec4>)` | statement | strip per-vertex colour | no
`submit_strip(p, count)` | statement | submits the first `count` strip vertices textured, with the pass's flat colour | no
`submit_strip_shaded(p, count)` | statement | submits them with per-vertex colours; <b>untextured</b> unless the preset consumes the texel (`TINT_MIX`) | no

@m_class{m-noindent}

Submission and strip calls cannot appear in expressions. Strip capacity is <b>8</b> vertices on the
PVR and <b>16</b> elsewhere; a <b>literal</b> index outside `0`..capacity-1 or count outside
`3`..capacity is a hard error (at runtime it would be silently dropped or clamped); computed values are
unchecked. Prefer fewer, longer strips --- on the GE each is a draw call.

@subsection shader-compiler-ff-target-lists Capability rules for a target list

A block naming several backends is validated against the <b>intersection</b> of their capabilities,
whichever backend is being generated.

Rule | On a target list
---- | ----------------
Extended vocabulary @m_span{m-text m-dim} (strip builder, quad axes, uniforms) @m_endspan | allowed; only the generic block is held to the portable core
`TINT_MIX` | only when <b>every</b> target is among `gx`, `pica`, `rdp`, `legacygl`; @cpp void fixed_function(gx, gu) @ce is rejected
`LUMA_RAMP` | only in a block targeting `gx` alone (the RDP combiner has no dot product)
`MODULATE_X2` / `MODULATE_X4` | rejected as soon as `gu` or `gs` is listed, even while generating the PVR header; `rdp` rejects `MODULATE_X4` only; `gx`/`legacygl`/`pica` have both
Strip-builder capacity | the <b>minimum</b> of the targets; @cpp void fixed_function(pvr, gx) @ce gets 8, so a literal index `8` is an error

@m_class{m-noindent}

Each diagnostic names the target that rejects the feature; the fix is splitting the list into separate
blocks.

@subsection shader-compiler-ff-statements Statements and expressions

Types: `float`, `int`, `bool`, `vec2`, `vec3`, `vec4` and the opaque `pass`.

-   <b>Statements</b> --- local declarations (initializers, comma-separated declarators), `pass`
    declarations, `=`, `+=`, `-=`, `*=`, `/=`, prefix/postfix `++` / `--` on an `int`, `if` / `else`,
    C-style `for` with an `int` counter and a `bool` condition, braced blocks with their own scope, and
    the submission/strip calls. Nothing else stands alone as a statement.
-   <b>Operators</b> --- `+ - * /` on numeric operands (scalar broadcasts over a vector, mismatched
    widths rejected); `%` on `int` only; `< > <= >=` on numeric scalars; `==` / `!=` on numeric scalars
    or `bool`s; `&& || ^^` on `bool`s; unary `-` on numeric, `!` on `bool`; ternary `?:` with a `bool`
    condition and same-typed branches.
-   <b>Functions</b> --- `min` / `max` on `(float, float)`, `(int, int)`, `(vecN, vecN)` or
    `(vecN, float)`; `clamp` and `mix` in the matching 3-argument shapes; `ceil` / `floor` on `float` or
    vector; `abs` / `sqrt` / `sin` / `cos` on `float`; `float(x)` / `int(x)`; `vec2` / `vec3` / `vec4`
    constructors as a scalar splat or any mix whose components sum to the width.
-   <b>Swizzles</b> --- single components in `xyzw`, `rgba` or `stpq`, plus `.xy`, `.zw`, `.xyz`, `.yzw`
    for reading. Only a <b>single component</b> can be assigned.

@m_class{m-noindent}

Rejected, each with its own error: `while`, `do`, `switch`, `return`, `break`, `continue`, `discard`,
arrays and indexing, unary `~`, `%=`, `&=`, `|=`, `^=`, `<<=`, `>>=`, assignments inside expressions,
initializing or assigning a `pass` as a value, redeclaring `COLOR` or `ctx`, and blend/tev preset names
as variables.

@subsection shader-compiler-ff-examples Worked examples

Shader | Shows
------ | -----
@cpp "WhiteMask.shader" @ce | the minimal shape: one silhouette pass coloured by the offset colour
`PartialWhiteMask.shader` | split by capability: offset-colour lift in `(pvr, gu, gs)`, x2 output scale on `gx`
@cpp "Outline.shader" @ce | a nested `for` over eight neighbours, each an offset silhouette guarded by `has_texel_size()`
@cpp "Colorized.shader" @ce | multiplier as up to three additive passes in `(pvr, gu, gs)`, two in `rdp`, one `MODULATE_X4` pass on `gx`
@cpp "FrozenMask.shader" @ce | constant ice tone where there is no per-texel arithmetic; `LUMA_RAMP` with `luma_gain = 2.6` on `gx`
@cpp "Transition.shader" @ce | iris fan from `quad_origin()` / `quad_axis_x/y()` and shaded strips; `pvr` gets its own block because the 10-vertex strip exceeds its 8
`Include/CombineWaterFixedFunction.inc` | shared `(gx, pica, gu, gs, legacygl)` block plus separate `rdp` (no light rays) and `pvr` (8-vertex strips) blocks

@m_class{m-noindent}

@cpp "Include/TexturedBackgroundWarp.inc" @ce shows the idiom for specializing a shared include: the
including block defines macros before the `#include`.

-   `WARP_TINT_IN_VERTEX_COLOR` folds the horizon tint into each band via `TINT_MIX`; without it the
    tint is a second alpha-blended pass after all bands.
-   `WARP_TEXTURE_REPEAT` draws each band as one strip with unwrapped coordinates on hardware that
    repeats the 256x256 render target (the PVR cannot); `WARP_TEXTURE_REPEAT_IN_PASS` also sets
    `p.texture_repeat` for the RDP.

The blocks: `(pvr, gs)` bare; `gu` repeat; `rdp` repeat in pass + tint; `pica` repeat + tint;
`(gx, legacygl)` tint.

@section shader-compiler-workflow Regenerating the committed headers

`--generate-all` enumerates every `*.shader` in @cpp "Sources/Shaders" @ce and writes into
@cpp "Sources/Shaders/Generated" @ce, in this order:

1.  @cpp "ShaderCompilerTypes.h" @ce --- shared reflection types (`--emit-types`)
2.  One header per `.shader` file --- `Default*.shader` into @cpp nCine::ShadersGen @ce, everything
    else into @cpp Jazz2::ShadersGen @ce
3.  @cpp "ShadersGen.h" @ce --- umbrella header plus the per-namespace `AllPrograms[]` arrays; a file
    with a `batched` twin contributes two entries
4.  @cpp "SwGeneratedShaders.h" @ce (`--emit-sw-generated`)
5.  @cpp "CgGeneratedShaders.h" @ce (`--emit-cg`)
6.  @cpp "PvrGeneratedEffects.h" @ce, @cpp "GxGeneratedEffects.h" @ce,
    @cpp "GuGeneratedEffects.h" @ce, @cpp "GsGeneratedEffects.h" @ce,
    @cpp "RdpGeneratedEffects.h" @ce, @cpp "LegacyGlGeneratedEffects.h" @ce,
    @cpp "PicaGeneratedEffects.h" @ce (`--emit-fixed-function`)
7.  @cpp "RsxGeneratedShaders.h" @ce (`--emit-rsx`), <b>only on a host with `cgcomp`</b>

@m_class{m-block m-info}

@par The PlayStation 3 header is skipped, not emptied
Without a runnable `cgcomp` (and NVIDIA's Cg Toolkit), `--generate-all` reports
@cpp "skipped: RsxGeneratedShaders.h" @ce and leaves the committed file untouched. Regenerate it on a
machine with the PS3 toolchain, or pass `--cgcomp`, see @ref shader-compiler-cg-rsx.

After editing a `.shader` file:

@code{.bat}
Sources\Utilities\ShaderCompiler\x64\Release\ShaderCompiler.exe --generate-all
git add Sources\Shaders Sources\Shaders\Generated
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

The shader directory is found by walking up from the executable, then from the working directory, to a
@cpp "Sources/Shaders" @ce; output goes to its @cpp "Generated" @ce subdirectory. Never regenerate
artifacts piecemeal --- it leaves the committed set inconsistent. `GenerateAll.ps1` is a thin wrapper
forwarding `-Glslang`, `-NoDxbc` and `-Check`:

@code{.bat}
cd Sources\Utilities\ShaderCompiler
powershell .\GenerateAll.ps1
@endcode

SPIR-V is embedded when `glslangValidator` is found: `--glslang <path>`, then `VULKAN_SDK\Bin` and
`VULKAN_SDK\Bin32`, then `PATH`, then a Visual Studio-bundled copy, then a repo-local build-tree copy.
Without it a warning is printed and the SPIR-V fields are null --- the headers build, but the Vulkan
backend does not.

`--generate-all --check` is the <b>staleness guard</b>: it generates into a temporary directory,
byte-compares with the committed headers (missing and extra files count), reports stale files and exits
non-zero without touching the tree. Without glslang or with `--no-dxbc`, every header with SPIR-V or DXBC
reports stale. Run it after editing a shader or in CI --- the build never detects stale headers.

Tests in @cpp "Sources/Utilities/ShaderCompiler/tests" @ce: `--check` dumps in @cpp "tests/expected" @ce,
must-fail inputs in @cpp "tests/errors" @ce, ESSL 100 fixtures in @cpp "tests/essl100" @ce;
`tests/RunTests.ps1` runs them all.

@section shader-compiler-editor Editor support (Visual Studio Code)

@cpp "Sources/Utilities/VSCodeExtension" @ce is a plain-JavaScript extension (no build step) for
`.shader` and `.inc` files. Its diagnostics come from `ShaderCompiler <file> --check`, so they match the
compiler exactly; it also provides highlighting, context-aware completion, hovers, an outline,
`#include` navigation, go-to-definition, snippets and transform previews.

@subsection shader-compiler-editor-install Installing

Symlink the in-tree copy (so it tracks the repository), then restart Visual Studio Code
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (or <em>Developer: Reload Window</em>) @m_endspan:

@code{.sh}
# Linux / macOS
ln -sfn "$PWD/Sources/Utilities/VSCodeExtension" ~/.vscode/extensions/death-shader
@endcode

@code{.bat}
rem Windows, from an elevated prompt (a directory junction needs no elevation on recent builds)
mklink /D "%USERPROFILE%\.vscode\extensions\death-shader" "%CD%\Sources\Utilities\VSCodeExtension"
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

To develop it, open the folder and press <tt>F5</tt> (@cpp ".vscode/launch.json" @ce opens the
repository root). `npx @vscode/vsce package` builds a `.vsix`; only that needs Node.

@subsection shader-compiler-editor-compiler How it finds the tool

First hit wins:

1.  The `deathShader.compilerPath` setting --- absolute, or relative to the workspace folder.
2.  @cpp "Sources/Utilities/ShaderCompiler/<x64|ARM64EC|Win32>/<Release|Debug>/" @ce, then any CMake
    tree at the repository root named `build*` or `cmake-build*`, then @cpp "out/build/<preset>/" @ce.
3.  `ShaderCompiler` on `PATH`.

@m_class{m-noindent}

The status bar shows the executable in use or warns when none was found; without one only the
@ref shader-compiler-editor-diagnostics "built-in checks" work.

@subsection shader-compiler-editor-features Highlighting and completion

Constructs the language rejects are highlighted as errors: `fragColor`, `#version`, unsupported canvas
built-ins, unknown render modes or uniform hints, and stage macros in `#if defined(...)` / `#elif` /
`#define` / `#undef`. Completion depends on the cursor:

Cursor position | Completion offers
--------------- | -----------------
Brace depth 0 | directives, entry points, `#include`, types, names declared in the file
After `shader_type`, `render_mode` or `precision` | only that directive's values
After the `:` of a `uniform` | only the seven uniform hints
Inside `void fixed_function(...)` parentheses | only `pvr`, `gx`, `pica`, `gu`, `gs`, `rdp`, `legacygl`
Inside a `fixed_function` body | the DSL statements, calls, pass fields and values, facilities, maths subset
Inside `vertex()` or `fragment()` | GLSL built-ins, mode-appropriate canvas built-ins, the file's declarations
Inside `#include "..."` | sibling `.inc` / `.shader` files

@subsection shader-compiler-editor-diagnostics Diagnostics

Two layers, both on by default:

1.  <b>The compiler</b> --- `ShaderCompiler <file> --check`; all three stderr shapes from
    @ref shader-compiler-usage "the command-line reference" are parsed.
2.  <b>Built-in checks</b> needing no executable: missing or duplicate `program`, missing
    `void fragment()` (or `void vertex()` in custom mode), a directive before `program`, `batched`
    without `shader_type canvas_item`, `#version`, `fragColor`, an illegal stage-macro form, `return;`
    in a canvas `vertex()`, an unsupported canvas built-in. Set `deathShader.validate.builtinChecks` to
    @cpp false @ce to leave diagnostics to the compiler.

@m_class{m-noindent}

Structural checks stand down in files with `#include` (as in @cpp "LightingMesh.shader" @ce) and in
`.inc` fragments. Since the compiler reports include-expanded line numbers, the extension notes the
expanded line in such files. Validation runs on open and save, or while typing with `onType`; a dirty
buffer is then written to a short-lived @cpp ".vscode-death-shader-tmp." @ce copy, next to the original
when it has `#include` lines, else in the OS temp directory.

@subsection shader-compiler-editor-previews Commands and settings

Command | Runs
------- | ----
<em>Death™ Shader: Show Reflection Dump</em> | `--check`
<em>Death™ Shader: Show HLSL Transform</em> | `--hlsl`
<em>Death™ Shader: Show Vulkan GLSL Transform</em> | `--vulkan`
<em>Death™ Shader: Show Cg Transform</em> | `--cg`
<em>Death™ Shader: Show ESSL 100 Transform</em> | `--essl100-check`
<em>Show Resolved ShaderCompiler Path</em>, <em>Validate Current File</em> | ---

Setting | Default | Meaning
------- | ------- | -------
`deathShader.compilerPath` | `""` | explicit executable path; empty auto-detects
`deathShader.validate.enable` | @cpp true @ce | report diagnostics at all
`deathShader.validate.run` | `onSave` | `onSave` or `onType`
`deathShader.validate.delay` | `400` | `onType` debounce in milliseconds
`deathShader.validate.builtinChecks` | @cpp true @ce | the executable-free checks

@m_class{m-noindent}

The extension is disabled in untrusted workspaces, since it executes a binary from the workspace.

@subsection shader-compiler-editor-maintaining Keeping it in step with the tool

<b>When this page gains a directive, `render_mode`, pass field or context facility, add it to
@cpp "src/language.js" @ce</b> (and to @cpp "syntaxes/deathshader.tmLanguage.json" @ce if it needs its
own colour). The dependency-free tests assert the closed sets against the parser:

@code{.sh}
node test/run-tests.js && node test/run-provider-tests.js
@endcode

@section shader-compiler-cg The Cg dialect (PS Vita and PlayStation 3)

Both consoles get Cg from the same emitter, but from different views: the Vita's with `LOW_POWER_GPU`,
the PS3's with `NO_DYNAMIC_BRANCHING` (see @ref shader-compiler-input-preprocessor).

-   <b>PS Vita</b> --- there is no offline GXP compiler (only the console's `libshacccg.suprx`), so
    @cpp "CgGeneratedShaders.h" @ce carries Cg <b>source</b> per program variant, compiled at load time
    through vitaShaRK.
-   <b>PlayStation 3</b> --- the console has no shader compiler, so the Cg is compiled to microcode
    offline, see @ref shader-compiler-cg-rsx "below".

Cg comes from @ref ShaderCompiler::HlslEmitter with
@ref ShaderCompiler::HlslEmitter::Dialect "Dialect::Cg". Differences from HLSL:

-   Both entry points are named `main`, not `VSMain` / `PSMain`.
-   Semantics are `POSITION` (clip position), `WPOS` (fragment position) and `COLOR` (output), not
    `SV_Position` / `SV_Target`.
-   No `cbuffer`: std140 block members become top-level uniforms. Samplers are `sampler2D` /
    `sampler3D` with the GXM `TEXUNIT<n>` semantic, read with `tex2D()` / `tex2Dlod()`.
-   No vertex-ID or instance-ID input: the ESSL 100 `VertexIdRewrite` turns `gl_VertexID` quad synthesis
    into reads of the `aQuadCorner` / `aInstanceIndex` attributes; a stage still using either built-in
    is rejected.
-   `BATCH_SIZE` is baked in as a plain `#define`; the backend rewrites the number when the runtime
    picks a different batch size.
-   A `mix()` of two vectors by a scalar casts the weight, @cpp lerp(a, b, ((float3)(t))) @ce, because
    the console's `SceShaccCg` finds @cpp lerp(float3, float3, float) @ce ambiguous for `tex2D()`
    results (Vita3K does not). HLSL keeps the plain call.

@subsection shader-compiler-cg-rsx Compiling that Cg for the PlayStation 3

`--emit-rsx` emits Cg like `--emit-cg` and then compiles it:

1.  <b>It compiles the plain view</b> --- `NO_DYNAMIC_BRANCHING` defined, `LOW_POWER_GPU` not. Low-power
    paths never reach the RSX, and editing only them leaves @cpp "RsxGeneratedShaders.h" @ce unchanged.
2.  <b>`BATCH_SIZE` is capped</b> to the batch the backend's corner stream covers, since instance arrays
    live in vertex-program constant registers rather than a 64 KB uniform buffer as on the Vita.
3.  <b>Each stage is compiled by `cgcomp`</b> (PSL1GHT) with the `vp40` / `fp40` profiles, found via
    `--cgcomp`, then `$PS3DEV/bin`, then `PATH`. "Unable to load Cg" means NVIDIA's `libCg.so` (Cg
    Toolkit) is not on the library path.

The header is a byte-array table looked up by (program, variant), plus the built-in <b>present</b>
shader written directly as Cg in @cpp "Sources/Shaders/Rsx/" @ce. A stage the profiles reject is
reported and left out, and that program fails at load time. The console build never invokes `cgcomp`;
see @ref consoles-ps3-shaders for the invocation.

@section shader-compiler-platforms Platform notes

-   <b>OpenGL|ES 2.0</b> (`NCINE_RHI_GL_PROFILE=ES2`, @cpp "#version 100" @ce) has no UBOs or
    @cpp gl_VertexID @ce, so the ESSL 100 lowering turns std140 blocks into loose uniforms or a uniform
    struct array and uses the `aQuadCorner` / `aInstanceIndex` attributes.
-   <b>Direct3D 11</b> --- `VSMain` / `PSMain`, std140 blocks as cbuffers, separate `Texture2D` +
    `SamplerState`, `mul()` matrix algebra.
-   <b>Vulkan</b> --- pipelines from the embedded SPIR-V, descriptor-set layout from the reflection.
-   <b>Fixed-function consoles</b> include their effect header from the device file under
    `WITH_RHI_PVR` / `WITH_RHI_GX` / `WITH_RHI_GU` / `WITH_RHI_GS` / `WITH_RHI_RDP`.
-   <b>PS Vita</b> (`WITH_RHI_GXM`) compiles Cg source on the console; <b>PlayStation 3</b>
    (`WITH_RHI_RSX`) loads precompiled microcode, see @ref shader-compiler-cg "above".

@m_class{m-noindent}

Console content is fully preprocessed (see @ref asset-packer "AssetPacker"), so runtime-compiled shaders
are desktop-only. See @ref consoles "Building for consoles" for those backends.

@section shader-compiler-pitfalls Pitfalls

-   <b>Never edit</b> @cpp "Sources/Shaders/Generated" @ce <b>by hand</b> (console headers included);
    the next regeneration overwrites it.
-   A layout change of @cpp "ShaderCompilerTypes.h" @ce invalidates every generated header --- one
    regeneration commit, guarded by `--check`.
-   A missing console effect means a missing block, not a failure --- read the generation summary.
-   `--emit-fixed-function` never reflects GLSL and `--check` never validates blocks, so each can pass
    while the other fails.
-   <b>Misspelled directives are not diagnosed</b>: unknown top-level keywords pass into the GLSL
    (`render_modee blend_mix;` fails in the GLSL compiler). Text after the `;` of a `varying`,
    `attribute` or `render_mode` is silently discarded.
-   Function-like macros are never expanded by the reflection preprocessor. Only one `BATCH_SIZE` array
    per block, as its last member. Struct-typed loose uniforms, sampler arrays, multi-dimensional arrays
    and array vertex attributes are rejected.

*/

}
